%[text] %[text:anchor:T_361B34F4] # Integration by Parts
%[text] [⇦ Main Menu](file:../MainMenu.m)
%[text] Integration by parts is an extremely powerful method of integration that will only come into full focus once you apply it to solving differential equations. For the moment, however, it will allow you to solve some integrals that substitution is no help with. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![An area u(b)\*v(b)-u(a)\*v(a) represented as the difference of two overlapping rectangles is divided into two parts by a curve. The upper area is the integral along the curve u(v)\*dv while the lower area is the integral along the curve v(u)\*du](text:image:1e02)
%[text] Integration by parts allows you to subdivide your integrand into integrated and differentiated components and recombine those pieces in ways that can be easier to compute.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"**Table of Contents**"}
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_51183E7B] **Before you get started:**
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BF646A47] This live script is intended to be used with the code hidden. On the **View** tab of the MATLAB toolstrip, in the **View** section, select **Hide Code**.  Alternately, select **Hide Code** using the icon ![live script code hidden icon](text:image:141e) at the top right of the Live Editor pane.
%[text] ![Lightbulb mark](text:image:3d16) Although the code is hidden, some interactivity requires familiarity with MATLAB. If you need more instruction, consider taking [MATLAB Onramp](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/matlab-onramp/gettingstarted), a free 2 hour online tutorial that teaches the essentials of MATLAB.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8C1C330D] ## Integration by Parts Geometrically
%[text]{"align":"center"} $u(b)v(b)-u(a)v(a) = \\int\_{v(a)}^{v(b)}u(v)\\;dv + \\int\_{u(a)}^{u(b)}v(u)\\; du \\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\; (3)${"altText":"u(b)v(b)-u(a)v(a) = integral from v(a) to v(b) of u(v) dv + integral from u(a) to u(b) of v(u) du (3)"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![An area u(b)\*v(b)-u(a)\*v(a) represented as the difference of two overlapping rectangles is divided into two parts by a curve. The upper area is the integral along the curve u(v)\*dv while the lower area is the integral along the curve v(u)\*du](text:image:96ae)
%[text] The left-hand side of equation $(3)${"altText":"(3)"} is the difference of the two rectangles while the right-hand side of equation $(3)${"altText":"(3)"} subdivides the same region into a sum of two pieces divided by the curve $(u,v)${"altText":"(u,v)"}. Practically, expressing $u${"altText":"u"} as a function of $v${"altText":"v"} or vice versa may be complex algebraically, but is straightforward geometrically using the parametrization in terms of $t${"altText":"t"}:
figure %[output:93743e3a]
syms t
u(t) = t^2; %[control:editfield:24a5]{"position":[8,11]}
v(t) = 1-cos(t); %[control:editfield:4ccb]{"position":[8,16]}
a = -5; %[control:slider:065d]{"position":[5,7]}
b = 5; %[control:slider:852b]{"position":[5,6]}
  %[control:button:2f55]{"position":[1,2]}
tl = tiledlayout(1,2); %[output:93743e3a]
if a < b
    tVals = a:.01:b;
else
    warning("You should choose a < b.")
    tVals = b:.01:a;
end
uVals = u(tVals);
vVals = v(tVals);
nexttile %[output:93743e3a]
plot(uVals,vVals) %[output:93743e3a]
title("Plotting $v$ as a function of $u$","Interpreter","latex") %[output:93743e3a]
xlabel("$u(t)$","Interpreter","latex") %[output:93743e3a]
ylabel("$v(t)$","Interpreter","latex") %[output:93743e3a]
axis square %[output:93743e3a]
nexttile %[output:93743e3a]
plot(vVals,uVals) %[output:93743e3a]
title("Plotting $u$ as a function of $v$","Interpreter","latex") %[output:93743e3a]
xlabel("$v(t)$","Interpreter","latex") %[output:93743e3a]
ylabel("$u(t)$","Interpreter","latex") %[output:93743e3a]
axis square %[output:93743e3a]
%%
figure %[output:65cfde1b]
syms t
u(t) = t^2; %[control:editfield:359c]{"position":[8,11]}
v(t) = 1-cos(t); %[control:editfield:8494]{"position":[8,16]}
a = 1; %[control:slider:0f35]{"position":[5,6]}
b = 3; %[control:slider:021a]{"position":[5,6]}
  %[control:button:8dc6]{"position":[1,2]}
if a>b
    disp("This code requires that b>a. Your endpoints have been reversed.")
    ar = b;
    br = a;
else
    ar = a;
    br = b;
end
tVals = ar:.01:br;
numSteps = length(tVals);
uVals = u(tVals);
vVals = v(tVals);
z = zeros(size(tVals));
p = plot(uVals,vVals); %[output:65cfde1b]
ax = p.Parent;
title("Geometric Integration by Parts") %[output:65cfde1b]
hold on %[output:65cfde1b]
xlabel("u(t)") %[output:65cfde1b]
ylabel("v(t)") %[output:65cfde1b]
v0 = double(v(0));
u0 = double(u(0));
[hx,hy,dUPosRegions,dUNegRegions,dULines] = PlotRegions(vVals,uVals,v0,["r" "b"],"h"); %[output:65cfde1b]
[vx,vy,dVPosRegions,dVNegRegions,dVLines] = PlotRegions(uVals,vVals,u0,["r" "b"],"v"); %[output:65cfde1b]
rectGrp = DrawRectangles(ax,uVals,vVals,u0,v0); %[output:65cfde1b]
surface([uVals;uVals],[vVals;vVals],[z;z],[tVals;tVals],... %[output:65cfde1b]
    "FaceColor","none","EdgeColor","interp","LineWidth",2) %[output:65cfde1b]
hold off %[output:65cfde1b]
SetXTicks(ax,double([u(ar) u(br)]),["u(a)" "u(b)"]) %[output:65cfde1b]
SetYTicks(ax,double([v(ar) v(br)]),["v(a)" "v(b)"]) %[output:65cfde1b]
%%
showPosdUReg = true; showNegdUReg = true; %[control:checkbox:220a]{"position":[16,20]} %[control:checkbox:2d39]{"position":[37,41]}
showPosdVReg = false; showNegdVReg = true; %[control:checkbox:80c3]{"position":[16,21]} %[control:checkbox:7854]{"position":[38,42]}
showRectSubs = true; hideVertStripes = true;    hideHorizStripes = true; %[control:checkbox:00d5]{"position":[16,20]} %[control:checkbox:2e85]{"position":[40,44]} %[control:checkbox:9614]{"position":[68,72]}
if showPosdUReg
    dUPosRegions.Visible = "on";
else
    dUPosRegions.Visible = "off"; %#ok<*UNRCH> 
end
if showNegdUReg
    dUNegRegions.Visible = "on";
